  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Degeneration of iris tissues; both the iris stroma and posterior iris epithelium can be affected, resulting in loss of iris sphincter and dilator muscle function, atrophy of iris vessels, and loss of iris pigment.",
              "pdf_page": 872,
              "printed_page": 790,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Both the pupillary margin and more peripheral portions of the iris can be affected, resulting in an iris that is thin or has areas of full‐thickness tissue loss.",
              "pdf_page": 872,
              "printed_page": 790,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Senile or secondary change.",
              "pdf_page": 872,
              "printed_page": 790,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Secondary iris atrophy is usually a result of chronic inflammation (uveitis).",
              "pdf_page": 872,
              "printed_page": 790,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Iris sphincter muscle is frequently affected by atrophy, resulting in incomplete pupillary constriction, unequal pupil size (anisocoria), and possibly abnormal pupil shape (dyscoria).",
              "pdf_page": 872,
              "printed_page": 790,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Irregular scalloped, moth‐eaten pupil margin is a common manifestation.",
              "pdf_page": 872,
              "printed_page": 790,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pupil margin may remain unaffected; peripheral loss of iris tissue can cause large holes in the iris that resemble multiple pupillary openings.",
              "pdf_page": 872,
              "printed_page": 790,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Vision largely unaffected.",
              "pdf_page": 872,
              "printed_page": 790,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
